            of its compactification
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi β
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta X
            as some kind of “ideal point at infinity”, and then we “freeze” (or localize) the variation by passing to germs of the variation at that point
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
          %p
            Notice that if we replace the word “ultrafilter” by the word “filter”, the general structural facts mentioned here would remain true. That is to say: if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is a filter consisting of subsets of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi colim
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi F
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} colim_{A \in F} \prod_{x \in A}
            (with such
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            ordered by reverse inclusion) is still directed or filtered, and we would still get a structure of given finitary type by passing to the colimit. Here, instead of ultrapowers and ultraproducts, one speaks of
            %em reduced powers
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %em reduced products
          %h3#ultraproducts_of_models Ultraproducts of models
          %p To be written.
          %h2#examples Examples
          %p
            The
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hyperreal+number"} hyperreal number
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hyperreal_numbers"} wikipedia
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonstandard+analysis"} nonstandard analysis
            are obtained as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/denumerable+set"} countable
            ultrapowers with help of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+ultrafilters"} free ultrafilters
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cofinite+subset"} cofinite
            subsets of integers, but not only them. See
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ultraproduct"} wikipedia:ultraproduct
          %p
            From Michael Barr’s
            %a{:href => "http://archive.numdam.org/ARCHIVE/CTGDC/CTGDC_1986__27_2/CTGDC_1986__27_2_93_0/CTGDC_1986__27_2_93_0.pdf"} Models of Sketches
          %blockquote
            %p
              Unlike
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limits"} limits
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimits"} colimits
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+mapping+property"} universal mapping property
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/filtered+colimit"} filtered
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/products"} products
              … But usually the category of models of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+theory"} coherent theory
              (such as the theory of
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+field"} fields
          %h3#in_functional_analysis In functional analysis
          %p
            (Appropriately-defined) ultrapowers of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Banach+space"} Banach spaces
            allow one to embed an infinite-dimensional space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ↪
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mi 𝒰
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \hookrightarrow X^{\mathcal{U}}
            inside a larger Banach space such that for every bounded family
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x_i)_{i \in I}
            and every ultrafilter
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒰
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{U}
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            = succeed "-limit" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒰
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{U}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi lim
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo ,
                      %mi 𝒰
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lim_{i, \mathcal{U}}
            exists (just take the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/germ"} germ
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [(x_i)]
          %p
            There are various ways to formalize this: Henson’s
            = succeed "," do
              %span.newWikiWord
                positive bounded logic
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/positive+bounded+logic"} ?
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+logic"} continuous logic
          %p
            As one might expect, things which are only “approximately true” inside
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            become “exactly true” in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mi 𝒰
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^{\mathcal{U}}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
            is an
            %span.newWikiWord
              approximate eigenvalue
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/approximate+eigenvalue"} ?
            (e.g. on the boundary of the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum+of+an+operator"} spectrum
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
            becomes a
            %em bona fide
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/eigenvector"} eigenvector
            with eigenvalue
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi T
                    %mi 𝒰
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T^{\mathcal{U}}
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mi 𝒰
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^{\mathcal{U}}
          %h2#related_entries Related entries
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+theory"} model theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ultrafilter+monad"} ultrafilter monad
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/large+cardinal"} large cardinal
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ultracategory"} ultracategory
          %h2#references References
          %p Standard references in model theory for ultraproducts are
          %ul
            %li#BellSlomson
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Bell"}> J. L. Bell
                , A. B. Slomson,
                %em Models and Ultraproducts: An Introduction
                , North-Holland Amsterdam 1969. (Dover reprint)
            %li
              %p
                P. C. Eklof,
                %em Ultraproducts for Algebraists
                , pp.105-137 in Barwise (ed.),
                %em Handbook of Mathematical Logic
                , Elsevier Amsterdam 1977.
          %p For a more recent textbook treatment see
          %ul
            %li#Hodges93
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wilfrid+Hodges"}> Wilfrid Hodges
              ,
              %em Model Theory
              , Cambridge University Press 1993. (sec. 9.5)
          %p The categorical perspective on ultraproducts goes back to
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                T. Okhuma,
                %em Ultrapowers in categories
                , Yokohama Math. J.
                %strong 14
                (1966) pp.17-37.
            %li
              %p
                S. Fakir, L. Haddad,
                %em Objets cohérents et ultraproduits dans les catégories
                , Journal of Algebra
                %strong 21
                (1972) pp.410–421.
          %p
            For the vacuum engineering point of view of ‘freezing variation’
            %sup#fnref:3
              %a{:href => "#fn:3", :rel => "footnote"} 3
            in the context of non-standard analysis see
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F.+William+Lawvere"}> F. William Lawvere
              ,
              %em Variable sets etendu and variable structure in topoi
              , Lecture notes University of Chicago 1975.
          %p
            The following papers are relevant for understanding the ultraproduct construction via
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codensity+monad"} codensity monads
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                D. P. Ellerman,
                %em Sheaves of structures and generalized ultraproducts
                , Annals of Mathematical Logic
                %strong 7
                (1974) pp.163–195.
            %li
              %p
                J. F. Kennison,
                %em Triples and compact sheaf representation
                , JPAA
                %strong 20
                (1981) pp.13-38.
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tom+Leinster"}> Tom Leinster
                ,
                %em Codensity and the Ultrafilter Monad
                , TAC
                %strong 28
                no. 13 (2013) pp.332-370. (
                %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/volumes/28/13/28-13abs.html"}> tac
                )
          %p For a fine point concerning the definition of ultraproducts:
          %ul
            %li#MathOverflow2012
              %p
                %a{:href => "https://mathoverflow.net/q/105397"} MathOverflow2012
            %li#Makkai1980
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M.+Makkai"}> M. Makkai
                ,
                %em On full embeddings
                , JPAA, 1980
          %p See also
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M.+Makkai"}> M. Makkai
              ,
              %em Ultraproducts and categorical logic
              , Lectures Notes in Math.
              = succeed "," do
                %strong 1130
          %p For ultraproducts in functional analysis see e.g.
          %ul
            %li
              Jose Iovino,
              %em Applications of model theory to functional analysis
          %p For a way to study ultraproducts in a homotopical setting see
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tobias+Barthel"}> Tobias Barthel
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tomer+Schlank"} Tomer Schlank
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nathaniel+Stapleton"} Nathaniel Stapleton
              = succeed "," do
                %em Chromatic homotopy theory is asymptotically algebraic
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1711.00844"} arXiv:1711.00844
          .footnotes
            %hr/
            %ol
              %li#fn:1
                %p
                  Due to the outstanding importance of the compactness theorem, it is possible to prove ‘almost all’ results in model theory by the use of ultraproducts. This approach to model theory is pursued in
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a{:href => "#BellSlomson"} Bell-Slomson (1969)
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a{:href => "#Hodges93"} Hodges (1993)
                  %a{:href => "#fnref:1", :rev => "footnote"} ↩
              %li#fn:2
                %p
                  The quotient notation is traditional but (ever so slightly) misleading. If all the
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi Y
                          %mi x
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y_x
                  are
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inhabited+sets"} inhabited sets
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                          %mrow
                            %mi x
                            %mo ∈
                            %mi A
                        %msub
                          %mi Y
                          %mi x
                        %mo →
                        %msub
                          %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                          %mrow
                            %mi x
                            %mo ∈
                            %mi B
                        %msub
                          %mi Y
                          %mi x
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prod_{x \in A} Y_x \to \prod_{x \in B} Y_x
                  (
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi B
                        %mo ⊆
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \subseteq A
                  ) are quotient maps and the ultraproduct is a quotient of the full product
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                            %mrow
                              %mi x
                              %mo ∈
                              %mi X
                          %msub
                            %mi Y
                            %mi x
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prod_{x \in X} Y_x
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi Y
                          %mi x
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y_x
                  is empty, then the full product is empty and thus the ultraproduct is not such a quotient. But it is useful to allow for empty models (when they exist)! The correct definition which works for all cases is as we have it: a filtered colimit over restricted products. See
                  %a{:href => "#Makkai1980"} p. 186
                  and
                  %a{:href => "#MathOverflow2012"} this MO Discussion
                  for a discussion of this topic.
                  %a{:href => "#fnref:2", :rev => "footnote"} ↩
              %li#fn:3
                %p
                  This perspective was apparently inspired by joined work with
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Miles+Tierney"} Miles Tierney
                  on the use of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/forcing"} forcing
                  in the context of the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuum+hypothesis"} continuum hypothesis
                  in early topos theory.
                  %a{:href => "#fnref:3", :rev => "footnote"} ↩
